 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Eliminates the requirement that certain minority and women-owned business enterprise owners have a personal net worth of less than three million five hundred thousand dollars in order to obtain certification as a certified minority and women-owned business enterprise; clarifies that an individual's net worth is to be considered in determining eligibility for certification under article 15-A of the executive law.",
 "title": "Eliminates the requirement that certain minority and women-owned business enterprise owners have a personal net worth of less than three million five hundred thousand dollars in order to obtain certification",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A2819",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A2819-2017",
     "printNo": "A2819",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 251\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2819, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the executive law, in relation to eliminating the\n      requirement that certain minority and women-owned business  enter-\n      prise  owners have a personal net worth of less than three million\n      five hundred thousand dollars in order to obtain certification  as\n      a  qualifying  minority  and  women-owned business enterprise; and\n      clarifying that an individual's net worth is not to be  considered\n      in determining eligibility for certification under Article 15-A of\n      the executive law\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  For  twenty-seven  years,  New  York  State's Minority and Women-owned\nBusiness Enterprise (\"MWBE\") program has continued to grow  and  thrive,\nproviding  significant  opportunities for MWBEs that, historically, have\nhad difficulties in obtaining state contracts and  other  opportunities.\nThroughout  my  tenure,  I  have  championed  the MWBE program, and I am\ncontinually looking to improve and strengthen opportunities  for  MWBEs.\nMy  efforts  are  reflected  in the increase to the State's MWBE partic-\nipation goal from 10 percent when I first took office to the current  30\npercent goal. In fact, utilization of the State's MWBE program is higher\nthan any other program in the country.\n \n  This bill would eliminate the requirement for certified MWBE owners to\nhave  a  defined personal net worth (\"PNW\") and prohibit the Director of\nthe Division of Minority and Women's Business  Development  (the  \"Divi-\nsion\")  from  considering an owner's PNW in determining his or her busi-\nness's eligibility for MWBE certification. Yet, as outlined below,  this\nbill  contains several fundamental flaws - which we sought to address to\nno avail with the Legislature - that make the bill legally specious  and\npractically unenforceable.\n \n  First,  this bill would unnecessarily expose the MWBE program to legal\nchallenge, because it would remove an essential component of the program\nwithout replacing it with a corresponding limiting factor. When New York\nestablished the MWBE program in 1988, it  was  challenged  on  constitu-\ntional  grounds just two years later. A federal court enjoined the State\nfrom implementing the MWBE program subject to a showing that there was a\n\"strong basis in evidence for its conclusion that race-conscious remedi-\nal action was necessary.\" Harrison & Burrowes Bridge Constructors,  Inc.\nv. Cuomo (Harrison & Burrowes I), 743 F. Supp. 977, 1001 (N.D.N.Y. 1990)\n(citing  City of Richmond V. J.A. Crosson Co., 488 U.S. 469, 500 (1989).\nUnder Croson, the United  States  Supreme  Court  held  that  government\nprograms  that  classify  individuals  on the basis of race, as does the\nMWBE program, must be  \"narrowly  tailored  to  remedy  prior  discrimi-\nnation...\"  City  of  Richmond  v.  J.A.  Croson  Co., 488 U.S. 469, 507\n(1989). To remedy the prior deficiencies in the  program  and  meet  the\nnarrowly  tailored standard, the Legislature identified several factors,\nincluding PNW, to serve as a basis to  identify  whether  a  MWBE  could\nparticipate  in  the  program  and  to  meet  the  standard's graduation\nrequirement. Notably, comparable programs lacking a PNW requirement or a\n \ncorresponding limiting factor have been struck down as unconstitutional.\nSee, e.g. Builders Ass'n of Greater Chicago  v.  City  of  Chicago,  298\nF.Supp.2d  725  (N.D.  Ill.  2003)  (enjoining the City's program as not\nnarrowly  tailored  because  \"[t]here is no net worth threshold. A third\ngeneration Japanese-American from a wealthy family, and with a  graduate\ndegree  from  MIT, qualifies (and an Iraqi immigrant does not).\"); Korn-\nhaas Const., Inc. v. Oklahoma Dept. of Central Services,  140  F.Supp.2d\n1232  (W.D. O.K. 2001) (enjoining Oklahoma program because \"the Oklahoma\nAct has no inherent time  limit,  and  no  provision  for  disadvantaged\nminority-owned businesses to \"graduate\" from preference eligibility\").\n \n  Second,  there  are  clear  technical deficiencies which make the bill\nimpossible to implement. Specifically, the PNW requirement is referenced\nin various sections of the current MWBE law.  However,  this  bill  only\nremoves  the  PNW  reference  in Executive Law Section 314 - it does not\nmake corresponding changes to other necessary Executive Law  provisions.\nThis  failure  results in contradictory interpretations of the program's\nPNW requirements, which not only undermine the Legislature's  intent  in\nadvancing  this bill, but expose the program and those administrating it\nto additional legal challenges in interpreting the law.\n \n  Recognizing these  fundamental  flaws,  I  made  numerous  efforts  to\ndiscuss  alternatives  with  the  Legislature.  In addition to proposing\nlanguage to correct the technical deficiencies, I also proposed substan-\ntive changes.  For example, I proposed creating another limiting  factor\nto  ensure  the  program would withstand constitutional scrutiny. I also\nproposed establishing a mechanism to identify a new method of  calculat-\ning the PNW requirement, such as utilizing a tiered approach to identify\nindustry-specific  PNW  levels  which  could  include  streamlining  the\nrequirements to establish PNW. However, the Legislature was unwilling to\nconsider or agree to any form of amendment to this bill.\n \n  Accordingly, given that enabling legislation for the MWBE  program  is\ndue to expire in 2018, and because the determination of PNW is an impor-\ntant  component  to  the  reauthorization  of  the  entire MWBE program,\ndiscussions regarding this issue should be considered in a comprehensive\nmanner during the 2018 legislative session,  when  we  expect  that  the\nlegislation  will  be negotiated. For these reasons, I am constrained to\nveto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 251,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2017
   }
